 ### [Dynamic Resolution Adaptation](2683-dynamic-resolution-adaptation.md)
-Dynamic Resolution Adaptation is a runtime performance management technique that adjusts the render viewport reduction factor based on previous frame render times and an allocated time budget to maintain a target frame rate. It solves the problem of performance instability in volume rendering, where small parametric changes (e.g., transfer function modifications) can cause frame rate dips leading to cybersickness. It sets itself apart from static resolution schemes by continuously shifting the "anchor points" between resolution levels based on the gradient of recent frame times, rather than using fixed blending factors. See [Dynamic Resolution Adaptation](2683-dynamic-resolution-adaptation.md).
