 ### [Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering](8163-focus-and-context-multiresolution-rendering.md)
-Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering is a technique that balances processing capacity between a high-detail "focus" region and a lower-detail "context" region to improve interactivity in time-critical applications. It solves the problem of limited hardware resources in volume rendering by decomposing the scene into two rendering passes with distinct parameters, rather than attempting seamless integration. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Level-of-detail (LOD) methods](1007-level-of-detail-lod-methods.md) by using a fixed spatial decomposition (lens) rather than geometric simplification, and from [Dynamic Volume Resolution & Alpha Mask Update](2539-dynamic-volume-resolution-alpha-mask-update.md) by relying on viewport scaling and sampling rate changes rather than data subsampling or alpha masking. See [Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering](8163-focus-and-context-multiresolution-rendering.md).
